From 7ec9ce9568a09cbb5bfacc5c0d06f8acd74600f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claudecode <hockinglee@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:01:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] perf(crates/base): keep clips listable without their
 pixels

Two things made a large clip history expensive.

`Entry::id` hashed the whole content on every call, and `list` calls it once
per clip, so opening the menu hashed every image in history -- over a gigabyte
of memory traffic for a few hundred screenshots. The identifier is now derived
from the digest, once, when the entry is built; it is also stable across
restarts.

A clip held its image as decoded pixels, four bytes each, so history kept every
image resident decoded and had to decode every one of them at start-up before
the daemon would answer. Only the clip being pasted needs pixels. `Entry` now
keeps the PNG encoding, and can stand for an image it does not hold at all:
`Entry::deferred_image` builds one from the digest and size recorded in the
history store, and `Entry::with_image` fills it in when the image is needed.
Identifier, mime, size, timestamp and preview are all answerable either way, so
listing history never reaches for an image.

`Entry::as_ref` gives way to `to_clipboard_content`, which decodes on the way to
the clipboard, and to `plaintext` for callers that only wanted the text.
Equality and hashing move to the digest, which an unloaded image also has.

PNG input is kept verbatim, only its header parsed, instead of being decoded and
encoded again; the digest stays the hash of the stored encoding, so existing
histories keep working unchanged.
